<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Sheikhdom of Zahir al-Umar</h1><p class="st-meta">1730 CE – 1775 CE (approx.) · capital Acre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The autonomous domain of Zahir al-Umar, a Bedouin sheikh who made Acre a thriving cotton port and defied Ottoman rule in Galilee.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zahir-al-umar">Zahir al-Umar</a> of the Zaydani clan was a tax farmer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>. From the 1730s he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>, and over the following decades he extended his control over Galilee and the coast, moving his seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> around 1750.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zahir-al-umar">Zahir</a> encouraged settlement, guaranteed security on the roads and brought in Jewish, Christian and Muslim settlers. He controlled the export of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and grain to French merchants, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> the leading port of the region, and rebuilt its walls. Though he usually paid his taxes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, he acted as an independent ruler and in 1771 joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-bey-al-kabir">Ali Bey</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in an attack that briefly took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1775, after the Ottoman fleet bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and his own men turned against him, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zahir-al-umar">Zahir</a> was killed. His successor in Acre, the Ottoman governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezzar-pasha">Jezzar Pasha</a>, continued his policies under direct Ottoman authority.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1730 CE – 1775 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.13%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1730 CE – 1775 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Acre</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zahir al-Umar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zahir al-Umar's Galilee, Zaydani sheikhdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1752&amp;focus=sheikhdom-of-zahir-al-umar" aria-label="Sheikhdom of Zahir al-Umar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5579 982 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sheikhdom of Zahir al-Umar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5899 1238L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Acre. <a href="/atlas?year=1752&amp;focus=sheikhdom-of-zahir-al-umar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

